---

<Base
  title="Why the CRM can be thrown away and rebuilt | Proof of Concept Bakery"
  description="Why Proof of Concept Bakery can drop its CRM's customer list and statistics and compute them again from a log, what makes that safe, and what it costs."
>
  <article class="essay prose">
    <p class="hint">
      <a href={HOW_PATH}>How it’s built</a>
    </p>
    <h1>Why the CRM can be thrown away and rebuilt</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The bakery’s customer list, timelines and campaign statistics can be emptied and computed
      again from a log while orders keep arriving, and the result is the same bytes as before. This
      is what has to be true for that to work, how the rebuild is paced, and what the lab shows of
      it.
    </p>

    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      On Monday morning someone finds that the rule counting a customer’s orders was wrong. Every
      customer row, timeline and campaign statistic derived with it is wrong too, and none can be
      edited into the right number; each has to be worked out again from what happened. Orders are
      arriving all the while.
    </p>

    <h2>What the lab shows, and what it cannot</h2>
    <p>
      The Backfill Lab, as read on {READ}, is a week of a nightly job counting a made-up bakery’s
      orders. A load fails halfway, and each day’s report is checked against the source. Its result
      is that a run which replaces its day’s rows in one step can run any number of times and leave
      the same report, while one that appends leaves 275 where the source has 179. That is the first
      half of this essay.
    </p>
    <p>
      The lab replays nothing from its event log, has no cursor or batch size, and has no object
      answering other requests meanwhile. It cannot show an alarm, a transaction in one object’s
      database, or a campaign send that has to wait. The bakery adds those.
    </p>

    <h2>What makes a projection disposable</h2>
    <p>
      The Crm object keeps two things apart. The projection is the customer list, timelines,
      activity feed, lead pipeline and campaign statistics, held as one stored row. The log is a
      table of every event that changed the projection, in the order applied. A projection is
      disposable only if folding the log again gives the same projection. Two rules make that so.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Applying an event is idempotent.</strong> The projection remembers the id of every
      event it has applied, and a second delivery is answered <code>duplicate</code> and changes
      nothing. Every application adds a line to a customer’s timeline, so a redelivered event would
      write its line twice, and the Queue delivers at least once. The count of a customer’s orders
      would survive, because it is worked out afresh from a table of orders on each change. The
      timeline and the bytes would not.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Applying an event is ordered per stream.</strong> For the streams of one order, one
      catering inquiry or one customer, the projection keeps the newest sequence number it has
      applied and ignores any event not newer. The Crm receives only some of each stream, so gaps
      are not waited for, but a late event with a lower number is answered <code>stale</code>. It is
      neither applied nor logged. That is what lets a replay reproduce the projection: the log is in
      applied order, and applying an entry depends only on what the projection holds at that point.
      Without the rule a late event would be applied and logged, and a timeline could move
      backwards, with an order shown as placed after it was shown as ready.
    </p>
    <p>
      Streams that carry many things, such as the inbox, need no sequence rule: their events add to
      sets and time-sorted lists, which come out the same in any order.
    </p>

    <h2>How the rebuild runs</h2>
    <p>
      A rebuild starts from the console’s Rebuild projections button, or the rebuild route of the
      API. The object keeps the projection as it stood, called the shadow, resets the live one to
      the roster, sets a cursor at the start of the log and arms an alarm. Each alarm replays the
      next 200 log entries in one transaction, saves the cursor and sets the next alarm 300
      milliseconds on. The batch size is <code>REBUILD_BATCH</code> in{' '}
      <code>crates/domain/src/crm.rs</code>, and the gap is <code>REBUILD_GAP_MS</code> in the
      object.
    </p>
    <p>
      The batches keep the object usable. Cloudflare’s documentation said on {READ} that Durable
      Objects are single-threaded and cooperatively multi-tasked, like code in a browser. The
      bakery’s reading, in ADR 0012, is that long work in one piece holds an object’s other requests
      behind it. Between runs of 200 entries the object answers them. Each run is also its own
      alarm, and an alarm handler had 15 minutes of wall time on {READ}, which a run of 200 entries
      does not approach.
    </p>
    <p>While a rebuild runs, four things hold.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Reads answer the partial projection</strong> and say so with the header{' '}
        <code>Projection-State: rebuilding</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A delivery is decided against the shadow,</strong> and if applied is appended to the
        log without touching the partial projection. The replay reaches it in its turn, and the
        cursor’s total grows to match.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A campaign send waits.</strong> It reads the projection to choose its recipients,
        and a partial projection would choose the wrong ones. The alarm logs{' '}
        <code>sends_deferred</code> and the send goes on the first alarm after the rebuild.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>
          A second rebuild is refused with 409 <code>rebuild</code>.
        </strong>{' '}
        It is the one command that cannot run during a rebuild. The same key again is a replay.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The proof is the bytes</h2>
    <p>
      At the end the object appends <code>ProjectionRebuildFinished</code> with the count of entries
      and the milliseconds taken, drops the shadow, and logs <code>rebuild_finished</code> with{' '}
      <code>identical</code>: whether the new projection’s serialized form equals the shadow’s,
      character for character. On 2026-11-05 the roadmap records the smoke script reading the
      customers, a timeline, the segments, the campaigns, the leads, the activity feed and the
      stored projection row before and after a rebuild of a 1,033-entry log, in batches of 200, and
      finding them byte-identical, with <code>identical: true</code> in the Worker’s log. The seed’s
      history is 722 closed-order events for seed 42 (
      <a href="https://github.com/IHaveASegway/bakery/blob/main/docs/roadmap.md">docs/roadmap.md</a>
      , PR 8, as of 2026-11-05). A property test of 256 cases delivers events in any order, with
      duplicates, and compares the projection with a fold of the log it applied.
    </p>
    <p>
      That shows the replay agrees with what it replaced, not that the rule is right: a bug in the
      rule is reproduced faithfully.
    </p>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <p>
      A rebuild reads each log entry once: 1,033 rows for the log above, in 6 runs, five of 200 and
      one of 33 (as of 2026-11-05). Each run also reads and writes the one row holding the
      projection. The bakery has not measured the rows Cloudflare bills for a rebuild, and the
      pricing page does not say whether a query that skips earlier rows counts them, so 1,033 is the
      least. For scale, on {READ} the paid plan included 25 billion rows read a month, and 1,033 is
      about four millionths of one percent of that. The wait is at least five gaps of 300 ms, 1.5
      seconds, before the work itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bound is the loop guard. An object that re-arms its own alarm more than 100 times in a UTC
      day stops arming until the wipe and logs <code>alarm_guard</code> with <code>rearm-cap</code>{' '}
      (<code>REARM_GUARD</code> in <code>stream.rs</code>; docs/operations.md, as of 2026-11-05).
      Each run is one re-arm, so at 200 entries a run the guard allows about 20,000 entries a day,
      less the outbox’s own re-arms. A longer log would, as the code reads, stall the rebuild
      partway until the wipe. A sandbox, wiped nightly, does not come near it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cost to a reader is the window: during a rebuild the customer list is partial, and the API
      says so with a header.
    </p>

    <h2>What to try</h2>
    <p>
      Open the{' '}
      <a href={labUrl('Backfill Lab')} rel="noopener">
        Backfill Lab
      </a>
      , choose Overwrite the day, fail a load, and rerun it; then choose Append rows and see the
      total pass the source. Then press Rebuild projections in the <a href="/app/">console</a>,
      watch the cursor climb in steps of 200, read the stats before and after, and try to start a
      second rebuild while the first runs. The{' '}
      <a href={howUrl('backfill')}>summary on How it’s built</a> says the same in short.
    </p>

